menu "Bluetooth bridge"
    config BRIDGE_BT_DEVICE_NAME
        string "Bluetooth device name"
        default "Dancefloor"
        help
            The name this bridge advertises, i.e. what your phone lists it as
            when you go looking for something to pair with. It is set on the
            stack once at startup, so changing it means a rebuild and a flash.

            Bluetooth carries the name as UTF-8 and the stack caps it at 32
            bytes, so a plain ASCII name has 32 characters to play with and
            anything accented or emoji has fewer.

            A phone that has already paired usually keeps the old name in its
            own cache. To see a new one, forget the device on the phone and
            pair again.

endmenu

menu "Bridge status LEDs"
    config BRIDGE_LED_CONNECTED_GPIO
        int "Connected LED GPIO (-1 to disable)"
        range -1 33
        default 32
        help
            Solid on while a phone is connected over A2DP, off otherwise.

            Wire the LED between 3V3 and the pin through a resistor (330 ohm is
            fine); the pin sinks it low to light it. See BRIDGE_LED_ACTIVE_LOW
            below for the other way round. Anything above GPIO 33 cannot be
            used -- 34-39 on a classic ESP32 are input-only.

            GPIO 13, 14, 15 and 25 belong to the SPI link to the hub and must
            not be used here.

    config BRIDGE_LED_STREAMING_GPIO
        int "Streaming LED GPIO (-1 to disable)"
        range -1 33
        default 33
        help
            Blinks at 0.5 Hz while A2DP audio packets are arriving, dark
            when the phone is paused or the stream has stopped. It follows the
            packets themselves, not the reported stream state, so a stalled
            stream goes dark even if the phone still calls itself playing.

    config BRIDGE_LED_ACTIVE_LOW
        bool "Status LEDs are wired to VCC (active low)"
        default y
        help
            Which way round both status LEDs are wired, which decides which
            level lights them. Set this and the driver writes 0 to light and 1
            to darken.

              y  anode to 3V3, cathode through a resistor to the pin. The pin
                 SINKS the current, so a LOW level lights it. This is how this
                 bridge is wired.

              n  anode from the pin through a resistor, cathode to ground. The
                 pin SOURCES the current and a HIGH level lights it. This is
                 what the GPIO help text above describes and what the firmware
                 assumed before this option existed.

            It covers BOTH LEDs, because they are on one board and one wiring
            convention; there is no case here for lighting one each way.

            Getting it wrong inverts rather than disables. The connected LED
            reads backwards -- lit when no phone is connected -- and the
            streaming LED still blinks, just in antiphase, which is invisible
            unless something else is blinking beside it. Neither failure looks
            like a wiring fault, which is why this is a setting rather than
            something to discover.

endmenu
